{
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "score": 4,
  "estimated_minutes": 25,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The necessary background is straightforward: Plotkin signed the NOLS student agreement, and his wrongful-death trustee seeks to keep the action in Minnesota while NOLS seeks transfer to Wyoming."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Understanding the Wyoming substantive-law clause requires distinguishing threshold choice-of-law rules, substantive contract interpretation, and federal forum-selection enforceability."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "low",
      "note": "The defects concern adjacent halves of one choice-of-law and forum-selection sentence, without requiring reconstruction of the enrollment documents or release provisions."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must reconcile the refusal to apply Wyoming law automatically at line 13 with its application to forum-clause interpretation at line 35, while separating those constructions from the other enforceability challenges."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The rejected ambiguity challenge to “any dispute” must be distinguished from the separately decided question whether “any mediation or suit” encompasses tort claims."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d01 — The reviewer must understand why selecting Wyoming substantive law does not itself select Wyoming rules for the threshold choice-of-law inquiry. Reading that holding alongside the later use of Wyoming law to interpret the forum clause requires keeping distinct legal inquiries separate.",
  "reason": "Assign this to a general litigation reviewer comfortable with basic conflicts-of-law distinctions; no wilderness-program expertise is needed. The main learning task is mapping which law governs which inquiry, rather than understanding the transaction. The tort-claim coverage and state-versus-federal forum constructions are expressly stated and readily recognizable. The reviewer should also note that d02 rejects an unexplained ambiguity assertion, rather than resolving competing meanings of “any dispute.”"
}
